WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.922
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.647 --> 00:00:14.480
PETER DER MANUELIAN: What does an expedition

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.700
leave behind for future generations?

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.120
Well, in the very old days, it wasn't really excavating,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.870
it was plunder and grabbed the treasures and go fill your museums.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:25.820
Thankfully, times have changed.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.740
And by about 1900, we had the era of responsible, scientific, modern

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.479
archeology underway.

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:33.720
What does that mean?

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.570
That means documentation.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.160
You're trying to keep a record of everything you do,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.840
so that future generations can virtually reconstruct

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.610
the results of your excavation.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.160
They can test your theories or interpretations,

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.840
and they can look at the records of your material and see if it all makes sense.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.870
George Reisner was one of the very first to do this,

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.510
following in the footsteps of his British counterpart

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.190
Flinders Petrie, who was making sequence records, annotations and taking

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:02.120
photographs.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.030
Reisner took all this a step further, and he worked out

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.490
a very complex recording system that tried

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:11.600
to capture all the information relevant to the expeditions

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.400
that he directed for so many years.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.000
So that began with photography, and in those days

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.800
it was glass plate negatives.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.000
Think of lugging a heavy wooden tripod and a large forman camera around.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.630
These negatives ranged in size from four by five inches,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.200
fairly small, all the way up to eight by ten inches.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.102
Imagine trying to keep that from breaking, not to mention deal

00:01:33.102 --> 00:01:34.810
with all the chemicals and get the things

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.370
stabilized when you're out in the dig field with the sand

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:40.820
and the wind and all those other challenges.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.900
So here is a photograph of the three different sizes of glass plate negative

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.970
that George Reisner used.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:50.510
In all of his digging years, so about 40 years

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:56.600
at 23 different archaeological sites, he amassed an archive of about 45,000

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.190
of these plates.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.730
For Giza, there are about 21,000 of them,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.860
and I'll return to that issue in a moment.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.010
What to do with these?

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.220
How to make them accessible?

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.000
So photographs are one challenge.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.880
And here is a shot of one of these large plate cameras.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.390
This is down in the tomb of Queen Hetepheres to G27000X.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.180
We're about 90 feet underground.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.490
So imagine getting all that equipment down there,

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:27.270
and the cables and the electric lights and setting up all the glass plates.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.780
Here's a photograph of the storage cabinets of some

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.840
of these tens of thousands of images.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.390
It is time that they're all made accessible.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:39.830
And that's part of what a project that I was very fortunate to set in motion

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.880
is trying to do.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.000
This began in the year 2000 with support from the Andrew W. Mellon Foundation.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.690
In addition to photographs of course, you need record books, ledgers.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.030
These were the databases of their time.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.560
Of course, Reisner didn't have access to modern SQL databases

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.780
and other types of relational information,

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.090
so it all had to be written in ledgers.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.340
There were photographic register books.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.290
They kept the numbers of the negatives.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:10.010
They kept the descriptions of the sites, what angle they were looking at,

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.980
what was the date, who was the photographer.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.210
And the same thing happened to the photographic object records of objects

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.740
as well.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.500
One of the challenges in preserving this material

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.950
is that the documentation is suffering sometimes almost as

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.050
much as the antiquities themselves.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:31.580
It has now been about 100 years or more since some of these great expeditions.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.590
So you're looking at two photographs of Giza

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.120
and they almost look like nuclear explosions here.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.560
But what you're really seeing is the chemical decomposition

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.020
of the chemical surface on the glass plates.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.430
They need to be conserved.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.660
This arrow is now pointing to a photograph of the famous pair

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.910
statue of Menkaure and his queen.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:55.290
Maybe you can just make out the shadow there as it comes into view.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:58.040
In addition to the glass plates and the photographic register,

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.750
there were object register books.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.870
These were huge ledgers that, again, were the databases

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.700
for all of the objects found.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.640
Everything inside had to be recorded precisely

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:11.050
and Reisner worked out a numbering system.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.990
Here's a sample page of a headrest.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.030
This would be a sort of ancient pillow.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.399
So this object would get its own number, it

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:21.390
would get a description, a sketch, the measurements, where did it come from.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.670
That's all important -- the provenance, right? --

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:27.730
What shaft, what tomb, what number did it come from?

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:29.950
And then of course, everything had to be described.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.430
So there were daily excavation diaries.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.230
Here's a page in Reisner's own hand.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.190
And the way he arranged it was to have a sort of table of contents

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.940
at the beginning of the page and then go into detail:

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.980
We did this tomb that tomb, this shaft, that temple,

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.190
and to describe what was found, how far down they went

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.720
and what they might have found.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.040
These are indispensable records of the daily progress of the expedition, not

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.900
to mention lots of local color.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.790
Visiting dignitaries and others who might

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:00.930
have come by the site, political events, some of the stormy international events

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:01.830
that happened.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.820
Remember, this is a man digging through two world Wars, one and two,

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:10.230
and trying to deal with his archaeological legacy accordingly.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.630
In recent years, we made an additional discovery.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.470
And that was by contacting the descendants of George Reisner's

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:17.760
Egyptian workmen.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.620
And it turned out they had saved about 74 excavation diaries

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.460
that their family members had been keeping in Arabic for George Reisner.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.440
For some reason, they had stayed in Egypt,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.950
and so we were able to get the permission to take these volumes

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.120
and to scan them.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.520
And in the case of the Giza books, we've translated them.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.440
And they will be part of our corpus of information.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:42.360
These are fantastic records that join our English diaries, and in many cases

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.440
they contain sketches that are missing from their English counterparts.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.040
In addition, an expedition has to leave plans and sections,

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.460
architectural drawings, renderings.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.560
Here is a shot of a major part of the Western cemetery.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.530
And you can see the mastaba tombs, large and small.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.920
All of their individual shafts have been given letter numbers.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.110
And you can see just how detailed and complex

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:11.020
it is to excavate an area as densely settled as this part of Giza is.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:14.050
There are plans and maps that are in excellent condition,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.670
depending on the types of paper used.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:17.170
There are others that are suffering.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:21.180
This is a kind of a cardboard like paper of a very, very large overview plan

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.950
that really wants to start crumbling to the touch.

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:27.760
So we've got the full range of conditions and preservation challenges

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.120
from the original expedition archive.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.660
George Reisner published a tremendous amount from his work

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:38.080
for the Harvard University Boston Museum of Fine Arts Expedition,

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.100
but he was mostly interested in interpretation and synthesis.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:45.940
So his volumes are often talking about casing types and burial shafts

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.280
and development of these different forms,

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:53.110
rather than treating each individual mastaba tomb by itself completely.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.450
He wanted to undertake such a series, but he did not

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.190
live long enough to do so.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.760
So it was left to his successors, the curators at the Museum of Fine Arts

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:04.570
Boston, to start something called the Giza Mastaba Series,

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:07.060
and to date there are eight volumes in this series.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.630
And they undertake the individual study of tombs in a one

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:14.160
by one sort of format.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.890
As much as George Reisner published, he left thousands

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.981
of pages of unpublished manuscripts.

00:07:18.981 --> 00:07:20.730
And this is something else that we've been

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.610
dealing with as we try to learn more about Giza

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.670
and unite all these different types of materials.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:30.660
And that's really the challenge, how to unite this diverse media.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.230
Here is a plan and a section drawing of an individual burial shaft.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.780
There are thousands of these left behind.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:40.470
From the graph paper pencil versions, to the final inked versions

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.270
meant for publication.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.630
And of course, figural drawings of the tomb scenes and inscriptions.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.670
Many of these have been drawn too, and they all

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.780
needed to be taken into account.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.390
Here is a scanning procedure of one of these oversized

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:56.340
and rather fragile drawings.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:59.670
So our big challenge then in attacking this material,

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.490
is, first of all, to be grateful that it exists because we have

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:05.100
this record of so much work at Giza.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.280
But then secondly, how do you make it accessible.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.260
In the old days even if you had a travel grant

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.270
to come to Boston to look at this archive, you would be overwhelmed.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:15.103
And chances are you didn't have enough money

00:08:15.103 --> 00:08:18.480
to stay long enough to view everything that you wanted to view.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:21.270
And so the challenge was to digitize, to scan,

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:25.620
to type, to database all this material, and most importantly to link it

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:30.300
together intelligently, so that online users anywhere in the world

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.030
could be interested in just about any part of Giza.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:37.950
From an inscription, to a statue, to a posture, to a tomb number,

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.159
to a particular photograph taken on a certain day.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.860
And all of that material should be accessible.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.680
This diagram shows you a mastaba tomb, that's

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:50.940
the individual tomb for an Old Kingdom elite Egyptian in the center.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.150
And then the arrows point to all the different types of media

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.670
that we've been dealing with since the year 2000,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.680
since the Giza project began.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:02.640
First at the Museum of Fine Arts Boston and now at Harvard University.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.310
So that means photographs, old and new, black and white

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:11.010
in color, diary entries, object register book entries, plans and sections,

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.280
published articles and books, unpublished manuscripts.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:18.090
All of it comes together and all of it gets linked to the appropriate tombs

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:21.150
and monuments in question.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:24.540
Along the way, we try to work with newer technologies as well.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.860
And in another section, I'll be talking about the 3D applications to Giza.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.590
From these traditional archaeological materials

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:35.520
we've then moved into 3D visualization of the entire site.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.270
And the goal now is to merge all of that material, old and new,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:43.620
so that you could virtually fly over the site, dive down a burial shaft,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:48.420
inspect a particular wall, and click on it to see a translation or a drawing

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.000
or a link to a publication about it.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.950
So this drawing that you're seeing now is

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:56.250
of a so-called slab stele, kind of a tombstone that was put

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.590
on the outside wall of a mastaba tomb.

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:02.290
It shows the deceased tomb owner, her name, her titles,

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:06.480
and it has various offerings and lists of linen intended for her burial.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.480
This was done without any pen or ink involved at all.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.560
But with a process I call digital epigraphy.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.570
The old days consisted of tracing.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.930
Here you see an Egyptologist with tracing paper, or actually

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.110
pencil tracing the inscription on Giza tomb wall.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.450
We don't work that way nowadays.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.380
We work from and enlarged photographs and try to trace the photographs.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:33.240
And then we've taken that step 1-bit further to move into the digital realm.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.450
And that means high resolution images onscreen

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.800
that we then trace with digitizing tablets and digital pens.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.860
So here's a beautiful photograph from the Reisner expedition.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:48.030
Again, preserving so much more than you might see on the tomb wall today.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:51.150
We then trace that on screen and create a beautiful vector

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:55.290
line drawing with wonderful mathematically calculated lines, not

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:57.180
pixels or bitmaps.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.340
And then we make the photograph go away.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:03.540
The result is a line drawing that can be scaled or re sized or published

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:07.200
or colorized or texture mapped onto a 3D model.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.270
We can even pull out individual hieroglyphs

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:13.050
and make paleographical charts to study how the shapes of the forms

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:18.540
changed from tomb to tomb and over time as the Old Kingdom progressed.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.080
So the Giza project is blending the older traditional archaeological

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.690
material with newer technologies.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:27.330
And in another module, I will show you some of the directions

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:31.310
that we're taking all of this exciting material further.

